---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import Breadcrumbs from '../../components/Breadcrumbs.astro';
---

<Layout
  title="Calculation Methodology & Architecture — FreightPlanHQ"
  description="Detailed technical breakdown of calculation architecture, unit normalization, packing algorithms, and source verification standards."
  canonicalPath="methodology"
>
  <!-- Top Breadcrumb Bar -->
  <div class="bg-canvas border-b border-hairline px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8 py-2.5">
    <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto">
      <Breadcrumbs items={[{ label: 'Methodology' }]} />
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="container-narrow py-8 sm:py-12">
    <header class="mb-8">
      <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 bg-canvas-elevated border border-hairline rounded-full px-3 py-1 text-xs text-mute mb-3 shadow-2xs">
        <span class="w-1.5 h-1.5 rounded-full bg-blue-500 inline-block animate-pulse"></span>
        <span class="font-mono uppercase tracking-wider font-semibold">ENGINEERING ARCHITECTURE</span>
      </div>
      <h1 class="text-3xl sm:text-4xl font-extrabold tracking-tight text-ink mb-3 leading-tight">
        Calculation Methodology & Architecture
      </h1>
      <p class="text-sm sm:text-base text-body leading-relaxed max-w-2xl m-0">
        How calculations, packing heuristics, and unit systems are architected across the platform.
      </p>
    </header>

    <div class="space-y-8 text-sm text-body leading-relaxed">
      <!-- Unit Normalization -->
      <section class="border-t border-hairline pt-6">
        <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-ink mb-2">1. Unit Normalization Engine</h2>
        <p>
          Calculations normalize all user inputs into canonical SI units internally before performing mathematical operations:
        </p>
        <ul class="space-y-1.5 pl-5 mt-2 list-disc">
          <li><strong>Length:</strong> Normalized to meters (m) using exact factors (1 in = 0.0254 m; 1 mm = 0.001 m).</li>
          <li><strong>Mass / Weight:</strong> Normalized to kilograms (kg) using exact factors (1 lb = 0.45359237 kg).</li>
          <li><strong>Volume:</strong> Normalized to cubic meters (m³) and liters.</li>
        </ul>
        <p class="mt-3">
          After calculations complete, values are converted back to the user's selected unit and formatted with locale-safe, context-appropriate decimal precision.
        </p>
      </section>

      <!-- Packing Heuristics -->
      <section class="border-t border-hairline pt-6">
        <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-ink mb-2">2. Rectangular Packing Heuristics</h2>
        <p>
          Unlike generic calculator websites that divide container volume by carton volume, our engines run deterministic 2D and 3D rectangular packing heuristics:
        </p>
        <div class="space-y-3 mt-3">
          <div class="border border-hairline rounded-xl p-4 bg-canvas-elevated shadow-xs">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-ink text-sm mb-1">Pallet Layer Geometry (TI × HI)</h3>
            <p class="text-xs text-mute m-0 leading-relaxed">
              Evaluates aligned and rotated rectangular floor patterns, mixed strip arrangements, and vertical layer constraints. Checks payload ratings, deck height allowances, and carton overhang limits.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="border border-hairline rounded-xl p-4 bg-canvas-elevated shadow-xs">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-ink text-sm mb-1">Trailer Floor & Pallet Packing</h3>
            <p class="text-xs text-mute m-0 leading-relaxed">
              Packs items across usable trailer widths (e.g. 2.4 m European trailers or 96" North American dry vans) to find the shortest continuous floor length (linear feet / loading meters).
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="border border-hairline rounded-xl p-4 bg-canvas-elevated shadow-xs">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-ink text-sm mb-1">3D Container Packing</h3>
            <p class="text-xs text-mute m-0 leading-relaxed">
              Evaluates multiple layer orientations and floor placements. Calculates actual Cartesian coordinate bounding boxes [x, y, z, l, w, h] to guarantee zero overlaps and verify equipment bounds.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- Density & Classification -->
      <section class="border-t border-hairline pt-6">
        <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-ink mb-2">3. Freight Density & Classification Boundary Logic</h2>
        <p>
          Freight class lookup utilizes the current NMFTA 13-sub full-scale density schedule. Boundary handling uses normalized half-open intervals: <code>min &le; density &lt; max</code>. For example, a density of exactly 15.000000 lb/ft³ is classified as Class 70 (15 to &lt;22.5 PCF), avoiding floating-point precision threshold bugs.
        </p>
      </section>

      <!-- Source Verification -->
      <section class="border-t border-hairline pt-6">
        <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-ink mb-2">4. Source Hierarchy & Verification Protocol</h2>
        <p>
          All reference specifications follow a strict source priority:
        </p>
        <ol class="space-y-1.5 pl-5 mt-2 list-decimal">
          <li><strong>Primary Standards Bodies:</strong> ISO (ISO 6780 for pallets) and EPAL.</li>
          <li><strong>Industry Associations:</strong> NMFTA (FCDC density schedules) and IATA (volumetric cargo conventions).</li>
          <li><strong>Equipment Specifications:</strong> Official container line specifications (Maersk, MSC, Hapag-Lloyd).</li>
          <li><strong>Carrier Service Guidelines:</strong> Published tariff rules from national parcel carriers (UPS, FedEx).</li>
        </ol>
      </section>
    </div>
  </div>
</Layout>
